\DOC LOCAL_MUL_4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MUL_4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Steps an {eventually}-style symbolic-execution goal forward over an inlined
4-word modular multiplication subroutine, replacing the current state by its
successor and recording the multiplication's result as new assumptions.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} giving the code-offset/instruction adjustment
used when locating the call site. The second is a {string list} naming the
result variable followed by the two operand variables of the multiplication:
the first name becomes the fresh variable for the product, and the other two
identify the inputs appearing in the derived congruence. The conclusion is
rewritten from {eventually ... s} to {eventually ... s'} for a successor state
{s'}, the predicate being otherwise unchanged. All assumptions describing the
old state — program-counter value, {bytes_loaded}, register and memory reads,
and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE ... s0 s} frame — are discharged and replaced by
the corresponding assumptions about {s'}, with the frame extended by the
components written by the subroutine; the pure arithmetic/side-condition
assumptions not mentioning the state are retained. Two further assumptions
about a fresh result variable {x1} are introduced, bounding it below twice the
modulus and asserting that it is congruent to the product of the two named
operands, along with a memory-read assumption giving {x1} as the contents of
the destination buffer in {s'} (part of {S(s')} below).
{
          A, S(s) ?- eventually P s
   =============================================================  LOCAL_MUL_4_TAC n ["x1"; "t"; "u"]
    A, x1 < .., (x1 == t * u) (mod ..), S(s') ?- eventually P s'
}

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, this is the only kind of forward step performed, always
on goals of this symbolic-execution shape.

\ENDDOC
